From 0fb3c10d6b029cb9b92ec7f0bbea2869b7748d72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 21:27:27 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Sun shadows: cascades that follow the camera, as Wind Waker
 HD's

The cascades were fixed to the sun and resized from HD's live splits every frame, and the facing term faded out from
12,000 units, so a far island (the title screen's) showed flat until the camera came near. Now, with the "hd" shadow
quality: each cascade is fitted to its slice of the view (near to split 0, split 0 to 1, split 1 to 4,500, 4,500 to
14,000); the terrain mix's fades follow the live splits by HD's rule; a far fade to lit from 12,000 to 14,000; and
the facing term no longer fades with distance (HD's per-vertex N.L doesn't).
DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_CASCADES_FOLLOW=0 keeps the old cascades, to compare.

Each cascade is fitted as Wind Waker HD fits its own (DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_CASCADES_HD=1, the default): the
tight box round its slice of the view, in HD's frame, refitted every frame. Read from Cemu captures of Outset's pier
turning: HD's first cascade is the tight box round the view's slice from 30 units to the first split, in a frame
whose y is the camera's forward made flat to the sun, with no steps or snap. At 21:9 the square about the sphere
round the slice wasted most of its texels: from Dale's boat at Outset the first cascade was 4096 units square (4 a
texel at 1024) where HD's fit is 3194 x 1038 (3.1 x 1.0; HD's own first cascade at the bridge, 2.4 x 1.3), the
second 13.5 square against 10.7 x 2.7. In play on Windows (Nov 10) the near shadows are sharper and nothing popped;
the cut where the characters' shadows end at the second cascade's end is still there.

_HD=2 takes HD's frame only, each cascade's size held in steps of 2^(1/4) with a margin before it shrinks and its
centre snapped to whole texels. _HD=0 gives the stable cascades: each cascade in the frame fixed to the sun, its box
the square about the sphere round its slice of the view, so the grid doesn't move as the camera turns (Dale tried
them in play on Nov 3: "the flicker isn't too bad"), at the cost of texels; with it,
DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_CASCADES_STABLE=0 turns the frame with the camera instead, its right made flat to the sun, the
sizes held and the centre snapped (and _TURN_STEP applies only then), where every shadow near the camera was drawn
on a new grid each frame of a turn.

Past the second cascade only the stage's BG lists cast, as in Wind Waker HD: its drawShadowCascade
(hd_layer_shadow.cpp) draws mpModelList into every cascade and the actor, effect and other lists only into the
first two. The host marks the game's draw list in BP register 0x7A (0 or never written: a BG list, 1: another),
kept until written again; the plan carries it as far_caster and the sun map's cascades from 2 on skip the others'
casters. The Tower of the Gods' six pillars no longer shade its far face, which HD leaves lit; Link's and the near
objects' shadows are unchanged. DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_FAR_CASTERS=all keeps every caster in every cascade.

DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_TEMPORAL_DEBUG=2: debug view 12 unblurred; the view also carries the pre-pass shadow in red.

On Linux, with the turned frame of the first version: the title's island is shaded from its first frames; the sword
state is bit-identical; Link on the pier within 0.3/255; GPU time the same with three cascades, about 0.1 ms for the
fourth.
